{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789969510,"nonce":"emn/hkwL224riSESZBQN9WFhx8k=","title":"The OSR frame block roots a transferred frame's live-ins for the rest of the call","message":"When an interpreted frame leaves at a loop header, the live-ins are\nwritten into a frame block the interpreter allocates through its\nScratch list, and that list is a root range until run_frame returns.\nThe compiled helper reads the live-ins out of the block once, but the\nblock keeps its words, so a live-in that dies inside the loop (a\nstructure the loop consumes and drops, or one rebound on every\niteration) stays reachable through the frame block until the whole\ncall returns.\n\nThe register file itself is unregistered at the transfer (see the\ncommit that closes the stale-root part of 89d16fa), so this is the\nremaining root the interpreter leaves behind. The fix is either for\nthe helper's prologue to clear the frame block after loading it, or\nfor the interpreter to release the frame block's registration while\nthe helper runs (the helper cannot be re-entered through it). Either\nway the words must be zeroed rather than merely unregistered if the\nblock is reused for another frame.\n\nShows as: ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=2 on a kernel whose main loop rebinds a\nlarge value each iteration reports one such value marked per\ncollection after the transfer; ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=3 names a root word\ninside the Scratch range.","files":null}]}